{"author":{"id":"ce771b8a9b8c30a8aac165a8b1cdb0957637d3941276ec4f27335daee16d79de"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794622555,"nonce":"J7ervu6ZwlJZiB5Kjo1PcVae24Y=","title":"Grilled 2026-11-13; supersedes the stub in this directory. All round","message":"questions answered (scope both sides, leptos_i18n, HA+browser locale\nchain, agent-drafted German with owner proofread, big-bang).\n\n\nThe app speaks English only, but its owner runs it inside a German\nHome Assistant with a German browser. Every label, form, banner, coach\ntip, and feed guidance reads as a foreign language, and environment\nalert notifications arrive in English too. The owner is gay, German,\nand the point of a private grow diary is that it reads like it was\nwritten for them — right now it was not.\n\n\nThe whole frontend renders in German or English chosen automatically:\nthe backend reports the Home Assistant user language, and the browser\nlocale is the fallback. Strings move out of inline literals into\ncompile-time catalogs (leptos_i18n) with the German catalog in du-form,\nauthored by the agent and proofread by the owner before release. The\nbackend localises its alert notification templates and the standard\nerror wrappers from a small catalog keyed by the Home Assistant\nlanguage. Key parity is enforced at compile time, browser-level German\nrendering is proven in the e2e suite, and nothing is fetched at\nruntime.\n\n\n1. As a German-speaking grower, I want the entire app UI in German, so\n   that my grow diary feels native.\n2. As a grower, I want the language chosen automatically from my Home\n   Assistant user language, so that I never touch a setting.\n3. As a grower, I want the browser locale as fallback, so that the app\n   still speaks my language when the HA language is unavailable.\n4. As an English-speaking user, I want English as the default, so that\n   nothing changes for unsupported locales.\n5. As a grower, I want form labels and select options in my language,\n   so that creating grows, strains, spaces, and batches needs no\n   translation guesswork.\n6. As a grower, I want banners and empty states localised (\"no data\",\n   \"no grows yet\"), so that warnings and states read true.\n7. As a grower, I want the phase coach tips localised, so that grow\n   science reads naturally.\n8. As a grower, I want lamp-height and VPD-band guidance localised, so\n   that the numbers keep their meaning and the sentences read native.\n9. As a grower, I want feed schedule guidance localised, so that the\n   feeding plan reads like my own notes.\n10. As a grower, I want plant-stage names and day-count phrasing\n    localised, so that the forecast speaks my language.\n11. As a grower, I want German plurals handled correctly (day counts,\n    batch quantities), so that \"1 Tag / 2 Tage\" reads right.\n12. As a grower, I want interpolated values (VPD bands, temperatures,\n    day counts, quantities) to keep working in both languages, so that\n    no string loses its numbers.\n13. As a grower, I want environment alert notifications in the language\n    my Home Assistant speaks, so that warnings arrive natively.\n14. As a grower, I want alerts to fall back to English when my HA\n    language is unsupported, so that alerts never arrive half-\n    translated.\n15. As a grower, I want API error banners (not found, bad request,\n    conflict) in my language, so that failures are understandable.\n16. As a grower, I want a du-form voice throughout, informal like Home\n    Assistant's own German UI, so that the app matches its home.\n17. As the owner reviewing, I want all German strings collectable in\n    one place per side (frontend locale files, backend catalog), so\n    that my proofread pass is one sitting.\n18. As the owner, I want EN↔DE key parity enforced at compile time, so\n    that German can never silently miss a string.\n19. As the owner, I want an e2e case proving German renders with a\n    German locale and English stays the default otherwise, so that the\n    §13 gate has browser-level evidence.\n20. As the owner, I want backend tests proving alert locale follows the\n    HA config language, so that the notification path is covered.\n21. As a grower, I want seed-data strings (strain names, breeder\n    fields) to stay untranslated, so that breeder names stay accurate.\n22. As a grower, I want unit displays to keep working exactly as today\n    (dual-unit), so that localisation changes no unit behaviour.\n23. As a grower, I want zero runtime fetches for translations, so the\n    app stays local-first and works offline.\n24. As a grower with an unsupported language, I want graceful English\n    everywhere, so that nothing is broken or mixed.\n25. As a maintainer, I want the UI-string extractor removed from the\n    prose gate (superseded by locale files), so that the lint stays\n    meaningful without false positives.\n26. As the owner, I want Home Assistant app-manifest metadata to stay\n    English this effort, so that the scope stays frontend + alerts.\n\n\n- **Frontend mechanism**: leptos_i18n 0.6.3 (requires Leptos ^0.8 —\n  verified against our stack; CSR-supported; compile-time macro).\n  Locale files as JSON inside the frontend crate; `en` is the default\n  locale and the key source of truth.\n- **Locale chain (frontend)**: the backend reports the HA user language\n  (the existing config fetch on every reconnect gains the `language`\n  field alongside the timezone it already publishes; a small locale\n  endpoint carries it to the frontend). Frontend order: HA language,\n  then `navigator.language` (`de*` → de), then English. No language\n  picker UI in this effort.\n- **Backend mechanism**: a small hand-rolled catalog module (const\n  template maps keyed by locale and message; `format!` interpolation)\n  covering the alert notification templates and the four standard error\n  wrapper phrases. Backend locale = the HA config language, English\n  fallback. Deep interpolated detail text (store/SQLite errors, entity\n  and category names) stays English by decision.\n- **Voice**: German in du-form throughout, matching Home Assistant's\n  own German UI.\n- **German authorship**: drafted by the agent; the owner proofreads the\n  frontend locale files and the backend catalog as a gate before the\n  release cut.\n- **Prose gate**: the UI-string extractor is removed from\n  `lint-prose` — locale text lives in catalog files the extractor\n  never reads, and Rust keeps keys, not literals. Markdown and Rust\n  comments stay gated; AGENTS.md wording updated to match.\n- **Manifest and app-title metadata**: English this effort, explicit\n  non-goal.\n- **Seed data**: never translated (evidence tags and breeder names are\n  data).\n- **Ship shape**: big-bang — one release where German exists fully;\n  incremental per-page mixes languages and is rejected.\n- **Decision log**: the mechanism and scope take the next free\n  D-number at implementation time (check the register top first).\n\n\n- Good tests assert external behaviour: what renders in German with a\n  German locale, what stays English by default, what language an alert\n  uses — never catalog internals.\n- **Compile-time key parity** is the coverage gate: the\n  leptos_i18n macro fails the build when a key is missing, so EN↔DE\n  parity cannot regress silently. A separate parity test is skipped as\n  redundant.\n- **E2e suite** (existing Playwright seam): a new spec forces the\n  German browser locale and asserts German renders (navigation, grow\n  page heading, a banner), plus default-English assertions; existing\n  conventions apply (ingress prefix helpers, no-reload guard where a\n  form is driven).\n- **Backend integration tests** (existing patterns): the locale\n  endpoint reflects the mock Supervisor's config language; an alert\n  renders German when the mock config declares `de` and English\n  otherwise. The mock Supervisor's config response gains a `language`\n  field (one line in the existing seam).\n- **Backend catalog unit tests**: template selection per locale,\n  interpolation, and English fallback.\n- No new seams: browser suite, backend integration tests, catalog unit\n  tests, and the compile gate — the same three families the grow-page\n  batch used, plus the macro.\n\n\n- Backend deep error-detail translation (store/SQLite texts, entity and\n  category labels).\n- Home Assistant app-manifest and app-title metadata.\n- A language picker in the UI.\n- Languages beyond EN and DE.\n- Runtime language switching without a browser/HA change.\n- Translating seed data or evidence tags.\n- The R1–R9 threat-model backlog.\n\n\n- The effort supersedes the stub previously in this directory.\n- Unit display is untouched: the app shows dual units today and keeps\n  doing so; localisation must not change units behaviour.\n- The D065 prose gate narrows to markdown and Rust comments; record\n  that narrowing in the decision entry alongside the mechanism.\n- German strings are agent-drafted; the owner's proofread is a release\n  checklist item for the release that carries this work, not a code\n  gate.\n- Root `GLOSSARY.md` gains the settled terms (locale source, key\n  parity, locale catalog) — keep UI text and decisions using them.","files":null}]}